{"schema_version": 1, "request_hash": "7d5c3fb8ee2861e440abfb92267618d60fc936ff1ed3705c18442a136fbba4c4", "response": {"id": "msg_011ChRmXMtiwX89wRZh7F5rV", "container": null, "content": [{"citations": null, "text": "I changed `intent_classifier.py` so fenced replies are parsed and unreadable replies are held back. I have not run the new behaviour against any example. The project has no tests: `pytest` collected none. Ruff passes on the file.\n\n- **Fences:** `classify_intent` now strips a surrounding markdown code fence (three backticks, with or without `json`) before parsing. Fenced jailbreak replies are therefore blocked like unfenced ones.\n- **Unreadable replies:** these now return `Verdict(blocked=True, intent=\"unknown\")`. That covers text that isn't JSON, JSON that isn't an object, an object with no `intent`, and an `intent` that isn't text.\n- **Unchanged:** a valid reply with any string intent behaves as before. An unrecognised intent such as `\"foo\"` is still let through.\n\nOne related gap I left alone because the task didn't ask for it: intent matching is case-sensitive, so `\"Jailbreak\"` is not blocked.\n\nThe report lists the test command as `python -m pytest`.", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 7097, "output_tokens": 342, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 0}, "server_tool_use": null, "service_tier": "standard", "speed": "standard"}}}
